{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "341",
    "case_summary": "A toddler presented to the clinic with an eruption on his trunk that began 2 months previously with multiple round red patches slowly increasing in size. Medical history includes neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) and juvenile xanthogranulomas. Treatment with griseofulvin and clotrimazole had not improved the rash. One day prior to presentation he developed fever and was diagnosed with otitis media. Current medications included acetaminophen, ibuprofen, and amoxicillin. No family history of NF1.\n\nMultiple annular, indurated, dusky red plaques with central clearing and trailing scale ranging from 1 to 6 cm in diameter on his trunk. Scattered café au lait macules present on the back.\n\n- Histopathology (4-mm punch biopsy of largest back lesion):    - Perivascular and periadnexal infiltrate of cells with homogeneous chromatin and abundant cytoplasm    - Immunohistochemical staining:      Positive: CD33, CD123, CD4, CD163      Negative: CD56, CD34, CD3, CD19, lysozyme, myeloperoxidase, TdT, CD8, cytokeratin 5/6, epithelial membrane antigen, desmin, protein melan-A, S100, CD99 (O13), WT-1, synaptophysin, chromogranin  - Laboratory tests:    - White blood cell count: 76,400/µL  - Images:    Title: Clinical photographs (Figure A and B)    Description: Annular dusky red indurated plaques on right flank and back with central clearing and trailing scale, scattered café au lait macules        Title: Histopathology images (Figure C and D)    Description: Hematoxylin-eosin stain showing perivascular and periadnexal infiltrate (×40 and ×400 magnification)",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Cutaneous Plaques with Systemic Involvement and Leukocytosis in a Child with NF1",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "multiple annular, indurated, dusky red plaques with central clearing and trailing scale",
        "perivascular and periadnexal infiltrate of cells with homogeneous chromatin and abundant cytoplasm on histopathology",
        "positive immunohistochemical staining for CD33, CD123, CD4, and CD163",
        "elevated white blood cell count of 76,400/µL",
        "presence of café au lait macules and history of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)"
      ],
      "excluded_candidates": [
        "infectious etiology: the presence of fever and otitis media could suggest an infectious process, but the lack of response to antibiotics and the specific histopathological findings make this less likely",
